            {
                "entry_id": "c6181e6f-a1ae-3c9f-b821-27dc0753a8bf",
                "word": "lemuroid",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "b1ef8bf7-dd11-4c50-acf2-6e3c16472f3f",
                        "entry_id": "c6181e6f-a1ae-3c9f-b821-27dc0753a8bf",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "lemuroids"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "65cd55b5-38b9-37db-9133-451ca9c016f5",
                        "entry_id": "c6181e6f-a1ae-3c9f-b821-27dc0753a8bf",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A member of the Lemuroidea superfamily of strepsirrhine primates, including lemurs"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3e2fc2cf-9f46-4df8-80e5-5fcfacc9f317",
                                "sense_id": "65cd55b5-38b9-37db-9133-451ca9c016f5",
                                "text": "In M. laniger the canine and most anterior premolar are more equal than in any other Lermuroid, or indeed than in any other Primate except Man."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bcde4d99-3cc2-4dde-973b-1a2eb6a2a031",
                                "sense_id": "65cd55b5-38b9-37db-9133-451ca9c016f5",
                                "text": "Supernumary or vestigal mammae may occur anywhere along the mammary line extinding from axilla to groin. Among lemuroids, however (cf. Shultz 1948), usually one but frequently up to three well-developed mammary pairs are pectoral (including axial), one pair abdominal and one or two pairs inguinal."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0a5a90f6-5d92-4999-8423-b5594d14b37c",
                                "sense_id": "65cd55b5-38b9-37db-9133-451ca9c016f5",
                                "text": "At a meeting of the Royal Society held on June 15, 1893, C.I. Forsyth Major descirbed the skull of an extinct Malagasy primate, the first to come to scientific attention (Major, 1894). Since that time, the subfossil remains of some six genera and 12 species of extinct lemuroids have been recovered in Madagascar, many of them represented by quite abundant material."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b10d856c-235e-37af-8ed1-a28cd61eaca2",
                        "entry_id": "c6181e6f-a1ae-3c9f-b821-27dc0753a8bf",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An animal that has the appearance or characteristics of a lemur"
                        ],
